"draft-irtf-cfrg-longfellow-00 - Longfellow ZK (https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-irtf-cfrg-longfellow/)\nciteturn7view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-irtf-cfrg-longfellow/\",\"pattern\":\"sumcheck\"}); Total lines: 3580\nL194:            4.4.4.  Ligero Prover procedure . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  27\nL195:          4.5.  Ligero verification procedure . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  30\nL196:        5.  Overview of the Longfellow protocol . . . . . . . . . . . . .  35\nL197:          5.1.  Parameters needed to define Longfellow  . . . . . . . . .  37\nL198:        6.  Sumcheck  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  37\nL199:          6.1.  Special conventions for sumcheck arrays . . . . . . . . .  37\nL1849:        The Longfellow ZK protocol uses two protocol components.  The first\nL1850:        is a variant of the sumcheck protocol, modified to support zero\nL1851:        knowledge.  Informally, the standard sumcheck prover takes the\nL1852:        description of a circuit and the concrete values of all the wires in\nL1853:        the circuit, and produces a proof that all wires have been computed\nL1854:        correctly.  The proof itself is a sequence of field elements.\nL1855:        Longfellow uses an encrypted-variant of the sumcheck prover that also\nL1856:        takes as input a random and secret one-time pad and outputs an\nL1857:        \"encrypted\" proof such that each element in this proof is the\nL1858:        difference of the element in the standard sumcheck proof and its\nL1859:        corresponding element in the pad.  (The choice of \"difference\"\nL1860:        instead of \"sum\" is a matter of convention.)\nL1861: \nL1862:     Frigo & shelat            Expires 22 April 2027                [Page 35]\nL1863:     Internet-Draft                Longfellow ZK                 October 2026\nL1864: \nL1865:        In this encrypted sumcheck variant, the verifier cannot check the\nL1866:        proof directly because it cannot access the one-time pad.  Instead of\nL1867:        running the sumcheck verifier directly, a commitment scheme is used\nL1868:        to hide the one-time pad, and the sumcheck verifier is translated\nL1869:        into a sequence of linear and quadratic constraints on the inputs and\nL1870:        the one-time pad.  A secondary proof system is then used to produce a\nL1871:        proof with respect to the commitment that the constraints are\nL1872:        satisfied.\nL1873: \nL1874:        The protocol requires both parties to agree on a circuit as part of\nL1875:        the theorem statement.  The wire format of a circuit is defined in a\nL1876:        separate document.\nL1877: \nL1878:        Some of the wires of the circuit are _inputs_, i.e., set outside the\nL1879:        circuit and not computed by the circuit itself.  Some of the inputs\nL1880:        are _public_, i.e., known to both parties, and some are _private_,\nL1881:        i.e., known only to the prover.  Sumcheck does not use the\nL1882:        distinction between public and private inputs.  This document\nL1883:        distinguishes private inputs from the one-time pad.  The commitment\nL1884:        scheme does not use public inputs at all, but it does treat private\nL1885:        inputs and the one-time pad elements equally.  These constraints\nL1886:        motivate the following terminology.\nL1887:        *  _public inputs_: inputs to the circuit known to both parties.\nL1888:        *  _private inputs_: inputs to the circuit known to the prover but\nL1889:           not to the verifier.\nL1895:           come first, followed by the one-time pad.\nL1896: \nL1897:        Thus, at a high level, the sequence of operations in the ZK protocol\nL1898:        is the following:\nL1899: \nL1900:        1.  The prover commits to all witness values.\nL1901: \nL1902:        2.  The prover runs the encrypted sumcheck prover on the witness\nL1903:            values to producing an encrypted proof, all-the-while sending the\nL1904:            encrypted proof to the verifier.\nL1905: \nL1906:        3.  Both the prover and the verifier take the public inputs and the\nL1907:            encrypted proof and produce a sequence of constraints.\nL1908: \nL1909:        4.  Using the commitment scheme and the witnesses, the prover\nL1910:            generates a proof that the constraints from step 3 are satisfied.\nL1911: \nL1912:     Frigo & shelat            Expires 22 April 2027                [Page 36]\nL1913:     Internet-Draft                Longfellow ZK                 October 2026\nL1914: \nL1915:        5.  The verifier uses the proof from step 4 and the constraints from\nL1916:            step 3 to check the constraints.\nL1917:        Steps 2 and 3 are referred to as \"sumcheck\", and the rest as\nL1918:        \"commitment scheme\".  While the classification of step 3 as\nL1919:        \"sumcheck\" is arbitrary, there are situations where one might want to\nL1920:        use a commitment scheme other than the Ligero protocol specified in\nL1921:        this document.  In this case, the \"commitment scheme\" can change\nL1922:        while the \"sumcheck\" remains unaffected.\nL1923: \nL1924:     5.1.  Parameters needed to define Longfellow\nL1925:        Longfellow is parameterized by a sumcheck protocol, a commitment\nL1926:        protocol, and a Fiat-Shamir instantiation.  A selection of all three\nL1927:        defines a Longfellow profile.  This document introduces one\nL1928:        opinionated profile that uses (a) The longfellow sumcheck described\nL1929:        below, (b) the Ligero commitment described above, (c) the Fiat-Shamir\nL1930:        instantiation defined above and using SHA-256 as the function H.\nL1931: \nL1932:        In addition to the component profile, the following security\nL1933:        parameters described in the Ligero Zero-Knowledge Proof Section\nL1934:        (#ligero-parameters) must also be selected:\nL1935: \nL1936:        *  NREQ: The number of columns of the commitment matrix in the Ligero\nL1937:           commitment scheme that the Verifier requests to be revealed by the\nL1938:           Prover.\nL1939:        *  rate: The inverse rate of the error correcting code used by\nL1940:           Ligero.\nL1941: \nL1942:        These two parameters are chosen to balance the size of the proof\nL1943:        against the soundness of the protocol.  In principle, these\nL1944:        parameters can differ based on the Field size.  Based on the latest\nL1945:        analysis, we support the following profiles which have been analyzed\nL1946:        to provide at least 115 bits of security.\nL1947: \nL1948:        *  (p256, 132, 7)\nL1949:        *  (GF(2^128), 132, 7)\nL1950: \nL1951:     6.  Sumcheck\nL1952: \nL1953:     6.1.  Special conventions for sumcheck arrays\nL1954: \nL1955:        The square brackets A[j] denote generic array indexing.\nL1956:        For the arrays of field elements used in the sumcheck protocol,\nL1957:        however, it is convenient to use the conventions that follow.\nL1958: \nL1959:     Frigo & shelat            Expires 22 April 2027                [Page 37]\nL1960:     Internet-Draft                Longfellow ZK                 October 2026\nL1961: \nL1962:        The sumcheck array A[i] is implicitly assumed to be defined for all\nL1963:        nonnegative integers i, padding with zeroes as necessary.  Here,\nL1964:        \"zero\" is well defined because A[] is an array of field elements.\nL1965: \nL1966:        Arrays can be multi-dimensional, as in the three-dimensional array\nL1967:        Q[g, l, r].  It is understood that the array is padded with\nL1968:        infinitely many zeroes in each dimension.\nL1969: \nL1970:        Depending on the context, some arrays may consist of almost all non-\nL1971:        zero values, while other arrays may be sparse, containing very few\nL1972:        non-zero values (ignoring the zero-padding convention above).\nL2178:        both the prover and the (virtual) sumcheck verifier agree on a claim\nL2179:        value, which represents the sum of the evaluation of some function at\nL2180:        all inputs {0,1}^*. The polynomials computed by the prover represent\nL2181:        the sum of the evaluations of the multilinear extension of that same\nL2182:        function, with one argument fixed to P0, P1, or P2, and all other\nL2183:        arguments chosen from {0,1}. Therefore, the sum of p(P0) + p(P1) is\nL2184:        equal to the claim from the start of the sumcheck round, and the\nL2185:        prover only needs to send two field elements in order for the parties\nL2186:        to agree on the entire degree two polynomial.  Here, p(P0) and p(P2)\nL2187:        are sent to the (virtual) sumcheck verifier, and p(P1) is\nL2188:        reconstructed from p(P0) and the claim.\nL2189: \nL2190:     6.5.  Transcript encryption and deferred verification\nL2191: \nL2192:        The sumcheck protocol produces a series of polynomials and claim\nL2199:        When the verifier executes the sumcheck protocol, it does not have\nL2200:        direct access to all the circuit inputs, and it is only given the\nL2201:        one-time pad encrypted forms of the sumcheck polynomials and per-\nL2202:        layer claims, not the corresponding plaintext values.  Therefore, the\nL2203:        prover and verifier defer part of the verification by producing a\nL2204:        series of linear and quadratic constraints, relating the private\nL2213:     Internet-Draft                Longfellow ZK                 October 2026\nL2214: \nL2215:        circuit layers in order, starting with the output layer (layer 0).\nL2216:        Within each layer, variables are first assigned to one-time pad\nL2217:        values for sumcheck polynomials, then to the per-layer claim values.\nL2218:        The number of sumcheck polynomials for each layer is equal to double\nL2219:        the value of logw for that layer of the circuit (two hands for each\nL3547:           -  c809ce332b7354d1e4b593f335d980dc1f0579e171a0bd092761a12006f3b544\nL3548: \nL3549:        *  *Commitment Root*:\nL3550:           30dafd53c4f2441bb8457cd84fa69b3a822fbda58a46527d5cec8ce1903bc504\nL3551: \nL3552:        *  *Sumcheck Proof Size*: 384 bytes\nL3553: \nL3554:        *  *Sumcheck Proof (hex)*:\nL3555: \nL3556:        *  *Ligero Proof Size*: 2632 bytes\nL3557: \nL3558:     Frigo & shelat            Expires 22 April 2027                [Page 71]\nL3559:     Internet-Draft                Longfellow ZK                 October 2026\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndraft-irtf-cfrg-longfellow-00 - Longfellow ZK (https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-irtf-cfrg-longfellow/)\nciteturn7view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-irtf-cfrg-longfellow/\",\"pattern\":\"Goldilocks\"}); Total lines: 3580\nNo matching text found for \"Goldilocks\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndraft-irtf-cfrg-longfellow-00 - Longfellow ZK (https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-irtf-cfrg-longfellow/)\nciteturn7view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-irtf-cfrg-longfellow/\",\"pattern\":\"Batch\"}); Total lines: 3580\nL889:        implied by nodes that have already been included.\nL890: \nL891:        To address these inefficiencies, this section explains how to produce\nL892:        a batch proof of inclusion for k leaves.  The main idea is to start\nL893:        from the requested set of leaves and build all of the implied\nL894:        internal nodes given the leaves.  For example, if sibling leaves are\nL895:        included, then their parent is implied, and the parent need not be\nL903:        necessary internal nodes of the Merkle tree that are used to verify\nL904:        the claim.\nL905: \nL906:        It is important in this formulation to treat the input digests as a\nL907:        sequence, i.e. with a given order.  Both the prover and verifier of\nL908:        this batch proof must use the same order of the requested_leaves\nL909:        array.\nL910: \nL911:        pub fn open_merkle_heap(\nL912:            mh: &MerkleHeap,\nL913:            leaf_indices: &[usize],\nL914:        ) -> Result<Vec<Vec<u8>>, &'static str> {\nL3261:        These vectors exercise the tree construction and the batch inclusion\nL3262:        proof only.  The leaves below are the leaf digests themselves, so no\nL3263:        nonces are involved; the nonce-blinded leaf construction used by the\nL3264:        Ligero commitment is exercised by the vectors in Appendix B.3, which\nL3265:        list their nonces explicitly.\nL3266: \nL3267:     Frigo & shelat            Expires 22 April 2027                [Page 65]\nL3268:     Internet-Draft                Longfellow ZK                 October 2026\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndraft-irtf-cfrg-longfellow-00 - Longfellow ZK (https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-irtf-cfrg-longfellow/)\nciteturn7view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-irtf-cfrg-longfellow/\",\"pattern\":\"github.com\"}); Total lines: 3580\nL76:     * 【56†Report a bug†github.com】\nL77:   * 【1†User】\nL78:     * 【57†Sign in】\nL79:     * 【58†Password reset】\nL80:     * 【59†Preferences】\nL81:     * 【60†New account】\nL82:     * 【1†List subscriptions】\nL83:       * 【61†IETF Lists†mailman3.ietf.org】\nL84:       * 【62†IRTF Lists†mailman3.irtf.org】\nL85:       * 【63†IAB Lists†mailman3.iab.org】\nL86:       * 【64†RFC-Editor Lists†mailman3.rfc-editor.org】\nL87: \nL88: Javascript disabled? Like other moder